{:edb/db-before-t 1310 :edb/db-after-t 1311 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534623 50 #inst "2026-10-12T05:04:12.000Z" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186115884 1071 "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" 13194139534623 17592186045417 false] [17592186115884 1071 "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\nII. $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n\nIII. The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186115884 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot k-2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6k+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $6k+6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true." 13194139534623 17592186045417 false] [17592186115884 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot k-2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6k+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $6k+6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true." 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186119932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429818 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186119934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429817 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186287749 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/3f/3fdfed680e3fb46adbe8a70aac86682aacb85d43e0675923f87658635fc89a1e.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/7f/7fbda500ecca7e18c9db9939ad78d4dcc6bf488bc7fc4de1137e764ce21a0254.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times R$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png)" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186287749 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/3f/3fdfed680e3fb46adbe8a70aac86682aacb85d43e0675923f87658635fc89a1e.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/7f/7fbda500ecca7e18c9db9939ad78d4dcc6bf488bc7fc4de1137e764ce21a0254.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times R$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png)" 13194139534623 17592186045417 false] [17592186342241 1074 "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t=1 \\\\ 4s-6t=-10 \\\\ 3s-7t=-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -6 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t=1 \\\\ -2t=-6 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2t=-6\\,⟹\\,t=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+t & =1 \\\\ -s+3 & =1 \\\\ -s & =-2 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=3,$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=2b+3c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot 3=6.$" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186342241 1074 "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-s+t=1 \\\\ 4s-6t=-10 \\\\ 3s-7t=-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -6 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-s+t=1 \\\\ -2t=-6 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2t=-6\\,⟹\\,t=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+t & =1 \\\\ -s+3 & =1 \\\\ -s & =-2 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=3,$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=2b+3c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot 3=6.$" 13194139534623 17592186045417 false] [17592186398576 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(t+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t)\\,j+{e}^{-t}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534623 17592186045417 false] [17592186398576 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(t+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t)\\,j+{e}^{-t}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\bbox[5pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429805 1080 "⟨-4,3,31⟩" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429805 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429805 1083 #uuid "20671737-03a7-5430-89bf-040b752377e1" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429806 1080 "64" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429806 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429806 1083 #uuid "3194d732-4a27-57e4-90b5-376278b1fad8" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429807 1080 "81" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429807 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429807 1083 #uuid "3c025708-e1f6-590a-bdc9-0995f51cbaec" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429808 1080 "⟨-3,5,31⟩" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429808 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429808 1083 #uuid "6c5b071d-27ac-57b7-af6d-728934e76426" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429809 1080 "⟨-5,3,31⟩" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429809 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429809 1083 #uuid "745f1be0-1559-5f77-9632-8cdde3498554" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429810 1080 "60" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429810 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429810 1083 #uuid "a745ff62-0494-5386-bded-3bb0f59f39cb" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429811 1080 "⟨-5,7,31⟩" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429811 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429811 1083 #uuid "cb172289-6398-5303-bbd8-83b5e2e9bc68" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429812 1080 "⟨-5,3,27⟩" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429812 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429812 1083 #uuid "dea73573-73e0-539b-8d97-0a1d8ac2f0eb" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429813 1080 "70" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429813 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429813 1083 #uuid "f1560774-e64f-5713-95be-58cfbace4e0f" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429814 1080 "53" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429814 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429814 1083 #uuid "fb522c79-1eec-53f4-a1ce-a41acd3ad978" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429815 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429806 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429815 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429807 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429815 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429810 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429815 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429813 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429815 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429814 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429815 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429815 1077 "selection" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429815 1078 #edb/ref 17592186429813 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429815 1079 #uuid "0b729052-de1a-56fa-9f79-cc8f868046e7" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429805 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429808 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429809 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429811 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429812 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429816 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429816 1077 "selection" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429816 1078 #edb/ref 17592186429809 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429816 1079 #uuid "e9b496fd-8c3a-561b-bf53-11d88d290ff5" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186429816 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429817 1069 "q-128493" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429817 1071 "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 17 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+s[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429817 1072 #uuid "3c3b23dc-4bcb-57b2-b0fc-dd67734d3e36" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429817 1074 "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨2,-7,1⟩$ and $w=⟨3,5,0⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-5i+3j+31k \\\\  & =⟨-5,3,31⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-5,3,31⟩.$" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186429815 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429818 1069 "q-158107" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429818 1071 "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429818 1072 #uuid "8d62e579-1b65-5746-996a-3e6e877f180c" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true] [17592186429818 1074 "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 & 13 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 & 13 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=(-7)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 1=-70.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-70|=70.\n$$" 13194139534623 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-20671737-03a7-5430-89bf-040b752377e1" 17592186429805 "answer-3194d732-4a27-57e4-90b5-376278b1fad8" 17592186429806 "answer-3c025708-e1f6-590a-bdc9-0995f51cbaec" 17592186429807 "answer-6c5b071d-27ac-57b7-af6d-728934e76426" 17592186429808 "answer-745f1be0-1559-5f77-9632-8cdde3498554" 17592186429809 "answer-a745ff62-0494-5386-bded-3bb0f59f39cb" 17592186429810 "answer-cb172289-6398-5303-bbd8-83b5e2e9bc68" 17592186429811 "answer-dea73573-73e0-539b-8d97-0a1d8ac2f0eb" 17592186429812 "answer-f1560774-e64f-5713-95be-58cfbace4e0f" 17592186429813 "answer-fb522c79-1eec-53f4-a1ce-a41acd3ad978" 17592186429814 "answer-field-0b729052-de1a-56fa-9f79-cc8f868046e7" 17592186429815 "answer-field-e9b496fd-8c3a-561b-bf53-11d88d290ff5" 17592186429816 "question-3c3b23dc-4bcb-57b2-b0fc-dd67734d3e36" 17592186429817 "question-8d62e579-1b65-5746-996a-3e6e877f180c" 17592186429818} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
